"Groups quantitative observations into adjacent intervals to show their frequency distribution.\n\nUse when: A continuous or finely discrete variable needs assessment of shape and tails. There are enough observations for bin summaries to be informative.\n\nAvoid when: A small sample would be represented more honestly with individual marks.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal intervals encode numeric bins. Bar height encodes count for equal-width bins, or density when widths differ.\n\nBuild: Select and disclose a bin width and boundary origin. Count observations using a consistent endpoint inclusion rule. Draw adjacent bars and label the frequency or density axis explicitly.\n\nCheck: Check bin counts sum to the included nonmissing sample size. Verify all equal-width bars share the same numeric width. Inspect a second reasonable bin width before interpreting modes.\n\nPitfalls: Bin choices can create or erase apparent clusters. Unequal-width bins require area, rather than height alone, to represent frequency.\n\nExample: Inspect synthetic parcel weights with 0.5 kg bins and count labels on the vertical axis.\n\nAlternatives: Variable-bin histogram, Density plot, ECDF."
